#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f767c5-7e54-5938-9d82-7c8b10472571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b36c722-da71-5e3c-9d5d-e002b8c67d3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "241dbed7-7afd-5efd-afb4-c20895ec0441" :question/math-academy-id "q-326014" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578432c0-a34d-58b0-80ea-88acb989d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245021} {:answer/id #uuid "3590535d-fdb1-5314-a9cd-d5c23ca08f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245022} {:answer/id #uuid "fabe72ec-9447-51e3-a477-6d231308242b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245023} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c7d16f-ef6a-5fb2-b94a-7d13756be6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245024} {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f66e357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f66e357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245025} :answer-field/id #uuid "27945f34-a1a4-5bda-8afb-726a7bef5b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42df49c5-88ed-5ab5-9dd5-9572a242592b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77515" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2p+4q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p+4q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$"}]}
